though it's technically continuation of  the blackops sub-series call of duty  black ops 4 kind of exists in its own  realm both multiplayer and zombies mix  old and new content in the new battle  royale mode blackout is an entirely new  direction for the series I recently  played black ops for a pre-launch event  covering the standard multiplayer  zombies and blackout I still want more  time with each mode as well as the  specialist training missions before I  give a final verdict so stay tuned for  the full review early next week  multiplayer is the most straightforward  of the modes and black ops 4 takes a  more tactical approach than previous  entries the wall running and thrust  jumping of black ops 3 is gone replaced  with a way grounded feel that forces you  to be a bit more thoughtful about  positioning healing is now manual to and  on a cooldown timer  matches overall have a slightly slower  more cautious pace but time to kill us  still low and frequent firefights keep  things exciting the mattes generally  stick to the familiar three lane  structure and are longer and narrower  than those in black ops 3 providing  opportunities for both close quarters  and long range encounters black ops  three specialists return with some  tweaks each still has a unique weapon  and ability but instead of choosing one  or the other you now go into a match  with both the weapon is tied to a longer  cooldown and functions like a super  while the ability is a piece of  equipment like a grenade or a trap with  a more strategic purpose some  specialists are more obviously geared  towards certain roles that is if they're  good for offense defense or support but  most modes don't give you much of a  reason to follow those guidelines if you  don't want to this isn't a hero shooter  and your success is more dependent on  effectively using your regular loadout  than your skill with a particular  specialist or your team's composition as  a result though the more tactical  aspects feel robbed of their impact and  committing to them isn't any more  rewarding than a normal kill  Zombies is as broad as it is deep with  two separate storylines across three  maps for if you have the blackops pass  included in the game special editions  the first two nine and voyage of despair  are part of the brand-new chaos story  the third blood of the Dead may be  joined by classified to make up the  returning either story it's a lot to  take in at once and I still need to  really dive into each map I'm most  intrigued by voyage of despair though  partially because of its Titanic setting  but also because it's tight spaces pose  a greater challenge than nines more open  arenas over in the either storyline the  Ultima screw has an age particularly  well even considering that each of them  is stereotyped to the extreme the jokes  just don't land anymore especially  Tokyo's overdone Japanese accent where  ELLs are replaced with ours at every  opportunity  suffering that you crave our you will  not be us aside from that slowly  uncovering the omage maps secrets is a  tantalizing prospect the new rush mode  is a much faster paced version of  zombies where you don't have to do any  thinking or puzzle solving you're just  there to kill the undead horde style  there's no money so you don't need to  save up to unlock a door either you're  instead told which area will have the  next rush wave and you're directed from  room to room as you go it's too intense  to be a tutorial per se but it's a great  way to familiarize yourself with the map  test weapons and maybe figure out which  rooms you should aim to reach first in  classic mode  since it's the brand-new load blackout  is obviously the most intriguing aspect  of black ops 4 but because there was a  limited number of people at the  pre-launch event I didn't get to play  many rounds or experience matchmaking on  live populated servers I want to spend  more time with the mode but as it's very  similar to pub G I also generally know  what to expect from blackout it  undoubtedly captures the intense shaky  excitement that makes battle royale so  popular with Call of Duty's tight  shooting and movement mechanics as a  strong foundation areas with zombies at  a PvE element that helps distinguish  blackout from the likes of pub g2  offering powerful loot at the risk of  dying from an undead mob or attracting  human players to your position perks and  specialist equipment also worked their  way into blackout as items that can be  looted during a match providing another  small but distinct Call of Duty twist  sure there isn't a traditional  single-player campaign but call of duty  black ops 4 isn't short on content and  its three main modes are substantial  with standard multiplayer in zombies it  struggles to fully commit to moving  forward and the mix of old content and  new ideas doesn't always gel from what  I've played so far black out more  successfully incorporates elements of  black ops past while forming its own  take on trends at the present but  because each mode is deep in its own  right I'll be spending more time with  black ops 4 post-launch before posting  my final review you might want to hold  him down  [Music]
 

